  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"terminal#9368\", \"terminal#16568\"], \"answer\": \"The send-input half of this has been reported before, and it was rejected. terminal#9368 asked for a `wt send-input` subcommand. The maintainers were wary of letting other processes inject input into terminal instances. They suggested an opt-in setting, but pointed out that settings.json alone can't be the gate because malicious.exe could edit it. In April 2026 [user] moved #9368 to the rejected state, and the community PR #20106 went with it. His reason was the security risk that mixed elevation opens up. terminal#16568 is an older, broader request for an iTerm2-style programmatic API. It is about controlling the terminal's structure, not reading buffers. I found no earlier ticket for the read side (session enumeration plus buffer read) or for the consent-gated, authenticated model you describe. That part may be new. Please link #9368 and #16568 in your issue and make the case for the differences: off by default, a UI-only toggle stored outside settings.json, per-process consent, no access to elevated windows, and read access separate from input. Today the only supported options are the structural `wt` subcommands (`new-tab`, `split-pane`, `focus-tab` and so on), the `sendInput` action bound in settings, and UIA. None of these can read a buffer or send input to a pane that is already running.\"}",
